      "dom_id": "question-64453",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=t,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{t\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}-2L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L\\{y\\}=Y(s).$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\}$ and $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$  we use formulas for the Laplace transforms of the derivatives and apply the initial conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\} & =sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s) \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\} & ={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{t\\}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}-2L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{t\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-2sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-2s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ (s-1)^{2}Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
