      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =2L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L\\{y\\}=Y(s).$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\}$ and $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$  we use formulas for the Laplace transforms of the derivatives and apply the initial conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\} & =sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s) \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\} & ={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =2L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-2)+sY(s)+Y(s) & =2(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}+s+1)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+2s+2}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+s+1)}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
