    {
      "dom_id": "question-345087",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-y(t)=-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+2s-5-Y(s)=-10(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1})+4(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+2s-5-Y(s) & =-10(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1})+4(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =5-2s-\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =\\frac{(-2s+5)({s}^{2}-1)}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{-2s+5}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1}-\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>1.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1}-\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\} \\\\  & =5{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\}-2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\} \\\\ y(t) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
