      "dom_id": "question-345079",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=4,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-s-4-4Y(s)=\\frac{4}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-s-4-4Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{s}+s+4 \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+4s+4}{s} \\\\ (s-2)(s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{(s+2)^{2}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{s(s-2)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>2.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\ y(t) & =2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\ y(t) & =2{e}^{2t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
